---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { SUBSCORES, SUBSCORE_LABELS } from '../lib/site';

const rubric: Record<(typeof SUBSCORES)[number], string> = {
  setup: 'How much work it takes to get going: onboarding flow, data import, documentation quality and how often users report a painful start.',
  scheduling: 'Calendar or dispatch board, recurring work, online booking, technician or staff assignment, and the mobile app used in the field.',
  invoicing: 'Quotes and estimates, invoices, card and ACH payments, deposits and accounting sync.',
  communication: 'Automatic reminders, on-my-way texts, two-way messaging, customer portal and review requests.',
  reporting: 'Revenue, job profitability, staff performance and whether the numbers an owner needs are available without exports.',
  value: 'What you get for the published price at the team size the product targets, including add-ons, per-user charges and contract terms.',
};
---

<ProseLayout
  title="How we review software"
  kicker="Methodology"
  path="/how-we-test/"
  updated={new Date('2026-09-26')}
  description="Every rating on ShopOwnerStack comes from the same six-part rubric, applied to vendor documentation, published pricing and patterns in public user reviews. Here is exactly how it works."
>
  <h2>What are our reviews based on?</h2>
  <p>
    Our reviews are research-based. For each product we collect four kinds of evidence:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Published pricing.</strong> Plans, user limits, add-ons and contract terms from the vendor's pricing page, cross-checked with recent third-party listings. The date we checked is shown on every review.</li>
    <li><strong>Vendor documentation.</strong> Help-centre articles and feature pages, to confirm what a feature actually does and which plan it is on.</li>
    <li><strong>Public user reviews.</strong> Rating averages and recurring themes in reviews on G2, Capterra, Software Advice, GetApp and TrustRadius, weighted towards recent reviews from small businesses.</li>
    <li><strong>Independent reporting.</strong> Trade press and industry data where it adds something the vendor will not say.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    We do not claim to have run timed tests or put live jobs through every product, and our pages
    do not imply it. Every review lists its sources so you can check them yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>How is the rating calculated?</h2>
  <p>
    Each product gets six sub-ratings out of 10. The overall rating is a weighted average in which
    value and the product's core workflow (scheduling for booking tools, invoicing for quote-led
    tools) count slightly more, because they are what an owner feels every week.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>What we look at</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {SUBSCORES.map((k) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{SUBSCORE_LABELS[k]}</strong></td><td>{rubric[k]}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What do the numbers mean?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>8.5 and above:</strong> outstanding for its target business; few meaningful gaps.</li>
    <li><strong>7.5 to 8.4:</strong> a strong choice with clear trade-offs you should check against your needs.</li>
    <li><strong>6.5 to 7.4:</strong> right for a specific kind of business, frustrating for others.</li>
    <li><strong>Below 6.5:</strong> significant problems; we would usually point you elsewhere.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Do affiliate commissions affect ratings?</h2>
  <p>
    No. Ratings are set before we look at whether a vendor has a partner programme, and products
    without one are reviewed and ranked the same way. Lists are ordered by rating, and the order is
    generated from the ratings automatically. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How often are reviews updated?</h2>
  <p>
    We re-check pricing whenever a vendor announces changes and at least every few months, and we
    revise a review when plans, features or the pattern of user feedback change materially. The
    "Updated" and "Prices checked" dates at the top of each page are the real dates of the last
    change. If you spot something out of date, <a href="/contact/">let us know</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
